{"subjectId":"sql","id":"case-expression","title":"CASE Expressions","group":"Querying Basics","order":19,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>CASE</code> is SQL's if/else. It is an <strong>expression</strong> — it returns a value and can be used anywhere a value can: <code>SELECT</code>, <code>WHERE</code>, <code>ORDER BY</code>, <code>GROUP BY</code>, inside aggregates.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-search\"></i>Searched CASE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name, salary,\n       CASE\n           WHEN salary &gt;= 120000 THEN 'Senior'\n           WHEN salary &gt;= 90000  THEN 'Mid'\n           ELSE 'Junior'\n       END AS band\nFROM employees\nORDER BY salary DESC, emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | salary | band\n-------+--------+-------\nAsha   | 150000 | Senior\nRavi   | 120000 | Senior\nPriya  | 110000 | Mid\nMeera  | 95000  | Mid\nKaran  | 95000  | Mid\nSara   | 85000  | Junior\nVikram | 80000  | Junior\nNeha   | 72000  | Junior\nArjun  | 70000  | Junior\nDev    | 60000  | Junior</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Conditions are tested top to bottom and the <strong>first</strong> true branch wins — so order the <code>WHEN</code>s from most to least specific. Without <code>ELSE</code>, unmatched rows get <code>NULL</code>.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-switch\"></i>Simple CASE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT order_id,\n       CASE status\n           WHEN 'delivered' THEN 'Done'\n           WHEN 'shipped'   THEN 'On the way'\n           WHEN 'pending'   THEN 'Processing'\n       END AS label\nFROM orders\nORDER BY order_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>order_id | label\n---------+-----------\n101      | Done\n102      | Done\n103      | Done\n104      | On the way\n105      | NULL\n106      | Done\n107      | Processing\n108      | On the way</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>A simple <code>CASE x WHEN NULL THEN …</code> never matches — it tests <code>x = NULL</code>. Use the searched form: <code>CASE WHEN x IS NULL THEN …</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sum\"></i>CASE inside aggregates: conditional counts</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT COUNT(*)                                           AS orders,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'delivered' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS delivered,\n       SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'cancelled' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS cancelled,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN order_date &gt;= '2024-03-01' THEN 1 END) AS in_march\nFROM orders;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>orders | delivered | cancelled | in_march\n-------+-----------+-----------+---------\n8      | 4         | 1         | 3</code></pre></figure>\n<p>This pattern — an aggregate over a <code>CASE</code> — is the basis of <strong>pivot</strong> queries (see Interview Prep). <code>COUNT(CASE … THEN 1 END)</code> works because unmatched rows produce <code>NULL</code>, which <code>COUNT</code> skips. PostgreSQL also offers <code>COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE status = 'delivered')</code>.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>CASE in ORDER BY and UPDATE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- different raise per band, in one statement\nUPDATE employees\nSET salary = salary + CASE WHEN salary &lt; 80000 THEN 5000\n                           WHEN salary &lt; 100000 THEN 3000\n                           ELSE 0 END;\n\nSELECT name, salary FROM employees WHERE salary &lt; 110000 ORDER BY salary, emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | salary\n-------+-------\nDev    | 65000\nArjun  | 75000\nNeha   | 77000\nVikram | 83000\nSara   | 88000\nMeera  | 98000\nKaran  | 98000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Short forms: <code>IIF(cond, a, b)</code> (SQL Server, SQLite), <code>IF(cond, a, b)</code> (MySQL), <code>DECODE(x, v1, r1, …, default)</code> (Oracle). <code>CASE</code> works everywhere.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Can you use CASE in a WHERE clause?</summary><div><p>Yes — it is just an expression: <code>WHERE CASE WHEN :role = 'admin' THEN 1 WHEN owner_id = :me THEN 1 ELSE 0 END = 1</code>. But plain <code>AND</code>/<code>OR</code> logic is usually clearer and friendlier to indexes.</p></div></details></section>"}